  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nToday Through Sunday: Trending Cooler with Some Storm Chances\n\nAn upper level low pressure system sitting over the northern \nCanadian Prairie provinces should slowly move east today. As \nthis occurs, a weak surface front is forecast to move southeast \nfrom the Dakotas towards the Upper Mississippi River valley. \nThis feature may be timed well with a weak shortwave on the \nsouthern periphery of the low to generate showers and storms \nahead of the front. Severe thunderstorms are possible across \nmuch of Minnesota, but the severe storm threat is much lesser \nacross our CWA. The timing of the frontal passage is not ideal \nfor strong instability as it moves over the region early \nSaturday morning. A line of showers/storms may form during this \ntime frame, but the severe storm potential will be quite low.\n\nMostly zonal flow is expected through this upcoming weekend and this \npattern should usher in a cooler airmass over the area. Temperatures \nfrom today through Sunday should steadily decrease, with highs \ntomorrow in the lower 80s to upper 70s and highs on Sunday in \nthe mid 60s to lower 70s. For those that are looking forward to \nFall, the weather will sure begin to feel like it this weekend! \nIn addition to the cooler temperatures, rain chances may start \nto ramp back up Sunday afternoon into the night. There are \ntiming and location differences in the forecast model suite that\ndoes lead to some uncertainty as to where the rain will set up \non Sunday. In any case, the rain showers or storms are not \nexpected to cause flooding at this time through Sunday night.\n\nMonday and Tuesday: Widespread Rain with the Potential for Flooding\n\nA stronger H500 trough will move over the Northern Rockies towards \nthe Northern Plains on Monday. With high pressure remaining over the \nsoutheastern CONUS, this will open the door for southwesterly flow \nto bring very rich Gulf of America moisture to the Upper Midwest. \nBlended ensemble forecast guidance continues to depict mean PWAT \nvalues approaching 2 inches throughout the day on Monday. A few \nrounds of rain are possible throughout the day, with the first \nlikely already ongoing at daybreak. The second round arrives a bit \nlater on Monday, mainly in the evening towards night time hours, as \na cold front moves east towards the region. The combination of both \nrounds of rain will likely result in a good portion of the area \nreceiving 1-2 inches of rainfall. Given the high moisture throughout \nthe atmosphere, the potential will exist for flooding and some of \nthe ensembles are hinting at this potential with the most extreme \nsolutions showing rainfall amounts of 3-5 inches by Tuesday morning. \nHow quickly the rain ends will be dependent on how quickly the drier \nairmass moves in behind the frontal passage, so it is not out of the \nrealm of possibility that some locations may continue to get rain on \nTuesday.\n\nRest of Next Week: Rain Chances Continue with Cooler Temperatures\n\nHigh temperatures through at least the middle of next week are \nexpected to remain in the 60s to 70s each day aided by the rain \nchances and increased cloud cover. Another potential for rain may \narise on Wednesday night into Thursday as mid level moisture \nincreases once again and potential shortwaves round the top of the \nupper level high over the southeastern US. With that being said, it \nwill certainly begin to feel like Fall with the increased chances \nfor rain along with cooler daytime highs and overnight lows \nthroughout all of next week.",
